WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.560
JoJo's Bizarre Adventure is one of&nbsp;
the most popular anime series around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.680
Fans especially love the villains, and rightly&nbsp;
so –- the rogues' gallery is as flamboyant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.680
and fantastic as its heroes. But&nbsp;
which villains reign supreme?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.640
We've ranked them from worst&nbsp;
to best, so let's take a look:

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.520
One of the many assassins dispatched by Dio Brando&nbsp;
to destroy the Star Crusaders in the third season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.760
J. Geil has a direct connection to&nbsp;
the heroes at the center of the story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.480
It's a dark one: He assaulted and&nbsp;
killed Jean Pierre Polnareff's sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.680
Sherry. This makes him a more central part of the&nbsp;
story than one might expect of a minor villain.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.920
There are two main characteristics that&nbsp;
make J. Geil stand out. The first is his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.480
distinctive physiology: he has two right hands.&nbsp;
The second is his Stand, Hanged Man, a potent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.480
light-based power that can jump from&nbsp;
one reflective surface to another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.440
J. Geil makes wicked use of his abilities,&nbsp;
killing protagonist Jotaro Kujo's friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.600
Mohammed Avdol, and nearly killing&nbsp;
Polnareff himself. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.040
he is defeated thanks to quick thinking and&nbsp;
manipulation of his Stand by Noriaki Kakyoin.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.080
Anjuro Katagiri is one of the creepiest Jojo bad&nbsp;
guys around. Appearing in Diamond Is Unbreakable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.800
he's a life-long criminal, convicted of robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.280
rape, and murder by the age of 12. He spent&nbsp;
the next 20 years in and out of prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.880
eventually landing himself on death&nbsp;
row. Just before his execution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.840
a mysterious stranger shot him with a bow and&nbsp;
arrow, which granted him the Stand Aqua Necklace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.640
which enabled his escape. He proves a formidable&nbsp;
foe for Jotaro Kujo and Josuke Higashikata — until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.520
they trick his Stand into being captured&nbsp;
and fuse the diabolical Angelo with a rock.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.120
His depraved criminality knows no bounds, whether&nbsp;
he's killing children or biting the face off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.400
dog. His Stand Aqua Necklace, made entirely of&nbsp;
water, is able to evade detection by merging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.520
with puddles and streams, change shape, and work&nbsp;
its way into the bodies of Angelo's victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.160
either possessing or outright killing them from&nbsp;
within. It's a gruesome power in the hands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.640
a gruesome man who, fortunately, falls&nbsp;
in battle early on in the heroes' quest.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.960
Daniel J. D'Arby, one of Dio's assassins,&nbsp;
is dispatched to defeat Jotaro Kujo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.280
While his stand, Osiris, allows him to steal&nbsp;
others' souls, he prefers to gamble for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.160
them in games of chance — games he inevitably&nbsp;
attempts to fix. It's only thanks to Jotaro's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.280
incredible ability to bluff in poker that&nbsp;
he's defeated and the souls he stole set free.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.680
What makes D'Arby stand out, other than&nbsp;
his excellent taste in vests, is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.360
unlike a lot of JoJo's Bizarre Adventure villains,&nbsp;
he isn't the type to be beaten in physical combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.240
His suave intelligence allows him to steal&nbsp;
the souls of half the Stardust Crusaders team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.680
and very nearly defeat Jotaro entirely.&nbsp;
He is, of course, bluffed into catatonia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.960
folding on four kings during a poker hand in&nbsp;
which the hero never once looks at his cards.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.000
"I can't do it! I'm petrified! I'm&nbsp;
too terrified to say the words!"

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.160
First of all, Steely Dan gets major props for&nbsp;
his moniker, which is an overt reference to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.960
jazz-influenced 1970s rock group of the same name.&nbsp;
But it's Dan's Stand, The Lovers, that really puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.240
him over the top. Microscopic in size, it crawls&nbsp;
into its victim's ear and attaches itself to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.520
brain stem, where it uses its power to cause any&nbsp;
attack on Steely Dan to be reflected back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.840
attacker, magnified. Oh, and it can also implant&nbsp;
Dio's flesh buds and murder its host from within.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.240
JoJo's Bizarre Adventure really builds up&nbsp;
viewer animosity for Steely Dan during his brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.320
appearance in Stardust Crusaders. By using The&nbsp;
Lovers on Jotaro's grandfather, Joseph Joestar, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.480
holds our hero hostage, threatening to hurt him if&nbsp;
he doesn't obey Dan's bidding. Humiliated, beaten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.440
and forced to rob a jewelry store while the&nbsp;
villain gloats, Jotaro is eventually able to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.280
Steely Dan pay, once his companions manage to free&nbsp;
Joseph from The Lovers. All this buildup makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.800
Steely Dan's defeat one of the most satisfying&nbsp;
dispatches of a bad guy in the show's entire run.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.160
Some characters in JoJo's Bizarre Adventure&nbsp;
possess Stands so powerful and impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.600
they strike fear into the heart of anyone&nbsp;
who has to face them. Terunosuke Miyamoto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.080
is not one of those characters. Even this&nbsp;
minor villain from Diamond Is Unbreakable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.080
admits that his Enigma power, which&nbsp;
allows him to turn someone into paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.040
if he can identify the mannerisms that&nbsp;
show they're afraid, is kind of lame.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.320
What makes this particular baddie so impressive,&nbsp;
though, is the mileage he manages to get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.280
of such an unimpressive Stand. When he&nbsp;
encounters Josuke and his companions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.480
he very quickly manages to learn their individual&nbsp;
expressions of fear, trapping the heroes in paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.920
form one at a time. Then, it's just a matter of&nbsp;
deploying a convenient paper shredder to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.800
perilous situation that is only narrowly averted.&nbsp;
Thankfully, Josuke and his team are saved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.840
and they exact a particularly fitting vengeance,&nbsp;
trapping Miyamoto in the form of a book, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.960
the hero then donates to a library. He's not the&nbsp;
most powerful villain a Joestar has ever faced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.200
but there's no better example of making&nbsp;
evil lemonade out of lemons, Stand-wise.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:12.320
Appearing in Stardust Crusaders, Hol Horse has the&nbsp;
mien of a cool gunslinger, and his Stand, Emperor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.120
is pretty awesome, taking the form of a pistol&nbsp;
with bullets he can redirect. However,, he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.400
one of the most ineffective and cowardly villains&nbsp;
in the long history of JoJo's Bizarre Adventure:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:28.960
Hol Horse runs away the moment he lacks villainous&nbsp;
backup. Hired by Dio Brando to assassinate Jotaro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.800
Kujo, he's several times a thorn in the side&nbsp;
of our heroes, but always ends up thwarted.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.120
So, why is he not at the top of the list?&nbsp;
Well, there are villains who scare you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.840
and villains who amuse you, and Hol Horse&nbsp;
definitely falls into that latter category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.240
His foolishness and unwillingness to,&nbsp;
pardon the pun, take a stand without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.680
backup leads to his plans falling through in&nbsp;
hilarious ways. Even his demise is ridiculous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.080
as his broken watch leads to him&nbsp;
messing up one of Boingo's prophecies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.520
firing Emperor into a pipe at the wrong&nbsp;
time and shooting himself instead of Jotaro.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.000
"The gun is mightier than the&nbsp;
sword! Huh! Such a brilliant quote!"

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.200
The list of Passione members who take on Giorno&nbsp;
Giovanna and Bucciarati's crew in Golden Wind is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.720
long, but none make quite as much of an impression&nbsp;
as this hot-headed, ice-wielding assassin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.720
His Stand, White Album, is not only named&nbsp;
after one of the best records of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:30.560
it's versatile and potent, and Ghiaccio knows how&nbsp;
to use it to deadly effect. It's simple, at root,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.200
allowing the wielder to freeze anything he&nbsp;
touches. But when Ghiaccio deploys it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.080
do everything from ice skate to solidify the&nbsp;
moisture in the air, it proves a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.520
challenge for Giorno to overcome. Oh, and it&nbsp;
also serves as impenetrable, full-body armor.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.920
Of course, like any great power, White Album&nbsp;
comes with a weakness: A small air hole at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.480
back of the neck. You might not think that would&nbsp;
be of enough significance to be exploitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:00.160
but it very much is. Fortunately for the good&nbsp;
guys, a conveniently placed splintered lamp post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.200
impales Ghiaccio and saves their hides. The killer&nbsp;
doesn't go out without a fight, though, attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.760
to freeze his own blood in order to stay alive&nbsp;
long enough to make one last-ditch attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.960
at victory. It's short-lived, and a chilling&nbsp;
conclusion for Passione's most memorable enforcer.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.840
A member of a powerful and ancient race of&nbsp;
superhuman beings known as the Pillar Men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.600
Kars is the main antagonist in the&nbsp;
second part of Battle Tendency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.240
He ticks a lot of the Big Bad boxes:&nbsp;
He's so evil he kills his own people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:32.320
he's super-powerful, and he has&nbsp;
insane goals of manipulating an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.120
ancient artifact –- the Stone Mask, which he&nbsp;
creates in an effort to maximize the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.920
of the Pillar Men's powers -– to become the&nbsp;
ultimate life form. Treacherous and relentless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.800
he wields considerable power in his quest to&nbsp;
complete the Stone Mask, including an effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.000
called Light Mode that creates chainsaw-esque&nbsp;
blades that can cut through solid stone.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.280
What makes Kars really cool, however, is the fact&nbsp;
that he actually achieves his goal. That's right:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.720
He really does manage to complete the Stone Mask&nbsp;
and don it to become the ultimate life form. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:08.240
only through a stroke of desperate instinct that&nbsp;
Joseph Joestar is able to redirect one of Kars'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.440
attacks in order to create a massive volcanic&nbsp;
eruption that launches the Pillar Man into space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.760
There, he freezes and drifts away from Earth,&nbsp;
doomed to forever float through the void.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:24.000
Vanilla Ice's name is one of the more on-the-nose&nbsp;
musical references in JoJo's Bizarre Adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.800
but make no mistake — this guy means business.&nbsp;
His most impressive characteristic is his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:34.320
slavish devotion to his boss, the vampire Dio&nbsp;
Brando. Let's face it, as a top-line villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.160
you can't do much better than a henchman&nbsp;
who is literally willing to slice off his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.200
own head to make you happy, as Vanilla Ice&nbsp;
does for Dio Brando in Stardust Crusaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.680
He's immediately resurrected as a vampire and&nbsp;
goes on to viciously attack the Crusaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.640
beating Iggy to death and nearly taking&nbsp;
out Polnareff, one piece at a time.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:57.280
When he's not busy sacrificing his life or&nbsp;
engaging in animal cruelty, Vanilla Ice wields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:04.000
the powerful Stand Cream, which, yes, is named&nbsp;
for the 1960s rock band featuring Eric Clapton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.520
Cream creates a sphere of void that swallows&nbsp;
its user, making him invincible and wiping out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:14.320
anything it comes across. While it does have&nbsp;
the weakness of making Ice unable to see while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:19.840
he's using it, Cream proves very potent, and it's&nbsp;
only thanks to Vanilla Ice's lack of experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:24.560
with his vampiric form that the Crusaders&nbsp;
are able to defeat him once and for all.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.920
Really, just look at this guy. Even among&nbsp;
the mighty Pillar Men, his physique stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.120
out — especially since Wamuu has no problem&nbsp;
showing it off in a variety of poses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.640
If his body is impressive, however, it's his&nbsp;
heart that really sets him apart. While he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.800
indisputably a villain, he possesses a clear moral&nbsp;
code and a respect for the honor that comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:51.840
battle. He's a man of his word, uninterested&nbsp;
in the immortality that motivates Kars and keen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.640
on respecting the enemies who prove themselves&nbsp;
against him. He also refuses to harm children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.800
which really makes him stand out in the&nbsp;
world of JoJo's Bizarre Adventure villains.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:07.280
Simply put, while Kars might be Battle Tendency's&nbsp;
biggest threat, Wamuu is its most interesting. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:12.240
uses his Divine Sandstorm power to defeat Joseph&nbsp;
Joestar, but accepts the hero's challenge of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:17.440
rematch a month later. Then, he faces Caesar&nbsp;
in a massively compelling fight that ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.560
thanks once more to Divine Sandstorm, in the&nbsp;
hero's demise. His final battle with Joseph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.960
a lengthy and violent chariot race, is one&nbsp;
of the more exciting moments in the series.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:33.680
The shadow Dio Brando casts over the events&nbsp;
of JoJo's Bizarre Adventure is huge: He's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.880
series' most important villain, the antagonist&nbsp;
for numerous generations of the Joestar family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.880
and quite possibly one of the nastiest bad guys in&nbsp;
anime. His list of transgressions prior to using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.920
the Stone Mask to become an immortal vampire&nbsp;
is heinous enough, but things only get worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.600
once he comes into his power. Said power includes&nbsp;
eye lasers, healing abilities, a freezing touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:02.000
and topping it all, his time-stopping Stand,&nbsp;
The World. Simply put, Dio Brando is bad news.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.280
He's also the main villain in two separate JoJo's&nbsp;
Bizarre Adventure series, first in his attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:13.280
usurp the Joestar fortune in Phantom Blood, and&nbsp;
then in a reincarnated form in Stardust Crusaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:18.160
Through it all, he displays a ruthlessness,&nbsp;
cunning, and bloodlust that sets him above and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:23.840
beyond every other bad guy in the series, taking&nbsp;
countless lives and wreaking endless havoc. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.800
and he's the reason the Joestars develop Stand&nbsp;
powers, which play a pivotal role in the show from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.760
the moment of their introduction. Every JoJo's&nbsp;
Bizarre Adventure villain has his or her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:39.680
importance to the story as a whole, but for sheer&nbsp;
impact and evil, there's no beating Dio Brando.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.720
"With my stand, I have the&nbsp;
ability to rule the world!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.680
What can you do in the face&nbsp;
of such overwhelming power?"

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.240
There's no beating Yoshikage Kira for&nbsp;
villainy. Unlike other names mentioned here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:57.120
he has no ambition to take over the world or&nbsp;
achieve immortality. Ultimately, he just wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.320
be left to his own devices — which include being&nbsp;
a serial killer and chopping off women's hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.160
Yes, he's a twisted soul, which makes&nbsp;
the fact that he possesses one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.960
most powerful Stands in the series all the&nbsp;
more horrifying. His explosive abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:15.920
all taking their name from Queen songs, allow&nbsp;
him to detonate his victims, either directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.720
in the case of Killer Queen, or remotely,&nbsp;
with Sheer Heart Attack and Bites the Dust.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:25.680
There are certainly more flamboyant villains in&nbsp;
the JoJo's Bizarre Adventure pantheon, but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.600
the juxtaposition of the mundane and the insane&nbsp;
that makes Yoshikage Kira one of the best around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:36.480
On the one hand, we spend time watching him live&nbsp;
a normal human life, taking walks in the park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.280
and being generally comprehensible. Then,&nbsp;
however, he indulges his most twisted tendencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:46.400
unleashing Killer Queen in pursuit of his sadistic&nbsp;
desires. He shows us the kind of monster that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:52.080
can exist under the most mundane of surfaces,&nbsp;
and that's why he's an unforgettable villain.

